{"id":"plagioclase","name":"Plagioclase","group":"Framework silicates","formula":"NaAlSi₃O₈ – CaAl₂Si₂O₈","chronometers":"Diffusion chronometry (Mg, Sr); crystal size distributions; ⁴⁰Ar/³⁹Ar","system":"Triclinic","point_group":"-1","setting":"C1̄ (low albite; anorthite P1̄ uses (h k 2l))","lattice":{"a":8.142,"b":12.785,"c":7.159,"alpha":94.19,"beta":116.61,"gamma":87.68},"lattice_source":"Harlow &amp; Brown (1980), low albite","elongation_axis":[1,0,0],"mono_color":"#d7dee6","view":{"tilt":16,"turn":-40},"forms":[{"key":"010","label":"{010}","index":[0,1,0],"name":"b, pinacoid (M; albite twin plane)","color":"#e9eef3","normals":[[-0.0086959,0.9999622,0.0],[0.0086959,-0.9999622,0.0]],"hkls":[[0,1,0],[0,-1,0]]},{"key":"001","label":"{001}","index":[0,0,1],"name":"c, pinacoid (P)","color":"#b9c6d3","normals":[[0.4465159,0.065376,0.8923842],[-0.4465159,-0.065376,-0.8923842]],"hkls":[[0,0,1],[0,0,-1]]},{"key":"110","label":"{110}","index":[1,1,0],"name":"m, pinacoid","color":"#d4dde7","normals":[[0.8673796,0.497647,0.0],[-0.8673796,-0.497647,0.0]],"hkls":[[1,1,0],[-1,-1,0]]},{"key":"1-10","label":"{11̅0}","index":[1,-1,0],"name":"M, pinacoid","color":"#a3b2c2","normals":[[0.8694992,-0.4939343,0.0],[-0.8694992,0.4939343,0.0]],"hkls":[[1,-1,0],[-1,1,0]]},{"key":"-101","label":"{1̅01}","index":[-1,0,1],"name":"x, pinacoid","color":"#8a9bb0","normals":[[-0.4340636,0.0658222,0.8984744],[0.4340636,-0.0658222,-0.8984744]],"hkls":[[-1,0,1],[1,0,-1]]},{"key":"-201","label":"{2̅01}","index":[-2,0,1],"name":"y, pinacoid","color":"#c7d1dc","normals":[[-0.8255103,0.0412365,0.5628786],[0.8255103,-0.0412365,-0.5628786]],"hkls":[[-2,0,1],[2,0,-1]]}],"basis":[[7.279292,0.063303,-3.646925],[0.0,12.750829,-0.934125],[0.0,0.0,7.159]],"reciprocal":[[0.13737599,-0.0,0.0],[-0.00068202,0.07842627,0.0],[0.06989284,0.01023326,0.13968431]],"axis":[0.8940423,0.0077748,-0.4479151],"measure_axis":[0.8940423,0.0077748,-0.4479151],"pg_order":2,"matrix":{"title":"(010) tabularity × elongation along [100]","subtitle":"Point group 1̄ makes every face its own pinacoid: m and M, and x and y, are independent forms. Rows flatten the crystal on b{010}; columns stretch it along [100] into the igneous lath.","row_title":"(010) tabularity (S/I)","col_title":"Elongation along [100] (I/L)","rows":[{"label":"S1","detail":"blocky"},{"label":"S2","detail":"0.6"},{"label":"S3","detail":"0.4"},{"label":"S4","detail":"0.25"},{"label":"S5","detail":"platy"}],"cols":[{"label":"I1","detail":"equant in (010)"},{"label":"I1.5","detail":"1.5"},{"label":"I2.5","detail":"2.5"},{"label":"I4","detail":"lath"}],"note":"Seven interfacial angles match Dana (1892) albite to within 0.25°, for example (010)∧(001) = 86.47° against 86°24′. The [100] lath axis follows common petrographic usage but was not confirmed from a primary source; [001]-elongated (anorthite-type) and [010]-elongated (pericline) habits also occur. Albite and pericline polysynthetic twinning cannot be shown as a single convex crystal."},"about":"\n<p>Plagioclase is triclinic, so its only symmetry is inversion and each pair of parallel faces is a separate pinacoid.\nThat is why m{110} and M{11̄0} can be developed differently. Igneous plagioclase is tabular on (010), and its 3D\nproportions (short : intermediate : long) record crystallization conditions: experiments relate morphology to undercooling\n(Lofgren 1974), 3D reconstructions show variable S/I at near-constant I/L (Duchene et al. 2008), and aspect ratio in\ndolerites tracks crystallization time (Holness 2014). Those 3D proportions are exactly the axes of this matrix.</p>","habits":[{"name":"Tabular on (010)","text":"The default habit, d(b) about half of d(c) (row S2)."},{"name":"Igneous lath","text":"Flattened on (010) and elongated along [100] (rows S3–S4, columns I2.5–I4)."},{"name":"Cleavelandite","text":"Platy pegmatitic albite (row S5)."},{"name":"Not modelled","text":"Pericline albite elongated along b, anorthite-type prisms along c, and all polysynthetic twins."}],"references":[{"citation":"Harlow, G.E., Brown, G.E. Jr. (1980). Low albite: an X-ray and neutron diffraction study. <i>American Mineralogist</i> 65, 986–995.","doi":null,"url":"http://www.minsocam.org/ammin/AM65/AM65_986.pdf","note":"The cell used here.","tags":["lattice"]},{"citation":"Lofgren, G. (1974). An experimental study of plagioclase crystal morphology; isothermal crystallization. <i>American Journal of Science</i> 274, 243–273.","doi":"10.2475/ajs.274.3.243","url":null,"note":"Plagioclase morphology (tabular → skeletal → spherulitic) versus undercooling.","tags":["morphology"]},{"citation":"Duchene, S., Pupier, E., De Veslud, C.L.C., Toplis, M.J. (2008). A 3D reconstruction of plagioclase crystals in a synthetic basalt. <i>American Mineralogist</i> 93, 893–901.","doi":"10.2138/am.2008.2679","url":null,"note":"3D short:intermediate:long proportions of experimental plagioclase.","tags":["geometry"]},{"citation":"Holness, M.B. (2014). The effect of crystallization time on plagioclase grain shape in dolerites. <i>Contributions to Mineralogy and Petrology</i> 168, 1076.","doi":"10.1007/s00410-014-1076-5","url":null,"note":"Plagioclase aspect ratio as a record of crystallization time.","tags":["morphology","chronometry"]},{"citation":"Costa, F., Dohmen, R., Chakraborty, S. (2008). Time scales of magmatic processes from modeling the zoning patterns of crystals. <i>Reviews in Mineralogy and Geochemistry</i> 69, 545–594.","doi":"10.2138/rmg.2008.69.14","url":null,"note":"Diffusion chronometry, including plagioclase.","tags":["chronometry"]},{"citation":"Angel, R.J., Carpenter, M.A., Finger, L.W. (1990). Structural variation associated with compositional variation and order-disorder behavior in anorthite-rich feldspars. <i>American Mineralogist</i> 75, 150–162.","doi":null,"url":"http://www.minsocam.org/ammin/AM75/AM75_150.pdf","note":"The anorthite cell, for the An end-member.","tags":["lattice"]},{"citation":"Smith, J.V., Brown, W.L. (1988). <i>Feldspar Minerals</i>, Vol. 1, 2nd ed. Springer.","doi":"10.1007/978-3-642-72594-4","url":null,"note":"The standard reference for feldspar crystallography and morphology.","tags":["review"]},{"citation":"Higgins, M.D. (1994). Numerical modeling of crystal shapes in thin sections: estimation of crystal habit and true size. <i>American Mineralogist</i> 79, 113–119.","doi":null,"url":"http://www.minsocam.org/ammin/AM79/AM79_113.pdf","note":"Recovering 3D habit from 2D sections.","tags":["geometry"]},{"citation":"Morgan, D.J., Jerram, D.A. (2006). On estimating crystal shape for crystal size distribution analysis. <i>Journal of Volcanology and Geothermal Research</i> 154, 1–7.","doi":"10.1016/j.jvolgeores.2005.09.016","url":null,"note":"Estimating short:intermediate:long shape for crystal size distributions.","tags":["geometry"]}],"cells":[{"id":"1a","row":0,"col":0,"label":"1a","sub":"","title":"1a: blocky, d(b)/d(c) = 1.0 · equant in the (010) plane","description":"blocky, d(b)/d(c) = 1.0; equant in the (010) plane.","default":false,"params":{"face_distances":{"010":1.0,"001":1.0,"110":1.0,"1-10":1.0,"-101":1.1,"-201":1.35},"cap_translation":0.0,"scale":0.5338521,"planes":{"010":0.5338521,"001":0.5338521,"110":0.5338521,"1-10":0.5338521,"-101":0.58723731,"-201":0.72070033}},"fractions":{"010":0.2457,"001":0.2247,"110":0.1784,"1-10":0.1765,"-101":0.1747},"metrics":{"volume":1.0,"surface_area":5.52303,"rsv":0.54318,"sphericity":0.8756,"aspect":0.9103},"geometry":{"vertices":[[0.31224,-0.53116,0.48091],[-0.05629,-0.53436,0.66555],[0.31224,-0.53116,-0.46383],[0.05629,-0.53338,-0.58732],[-0.61472,-0.00131,0.35671],[-0.30764,-0.53655,0.54428],[-0.61472,-0.00131,-0.29055],[-0.30764,-0.53655,-0.40499],[-0.31224,0.53116,-0.48091],[0.05629,0.53436,-0.66555],[-0.31224,0.53116,0.46383],[-0.05629,0.53338,0.58732],[0.30764,0.53655,-0.54428],[0.61472,0.00131,-0.35671],[0.61472,0.00131,0.29055],[0.30764,0.53655,0.40499]],"faces":[{"form":"010","hkl":[0,1,0],"loop":[12,9,8,10,11,15]},{"form":"010","h